EXT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - DAY

Oak Creek Elementary sits under a bright Ohio sun. Red brick walls glow. A brass sign reads: Oak Creek Elementary, Founded 1924.

INT. CLASSROOM - DAY

KABIR (10) sits at a wooden desk. He wears a green bomber jacket over a white tee. His canvas satchel hangs on his chair. It is covered in colorful patches.

He holds a folding wooden ruler. Click. Clack. He folds it open and shut.

MR. HENDERSON (40s) stands at the blackboard. He holds up a beautiful vintage brass compass. It gleams.

MR. HENDERSON
Today begins our annual Oak Creek history hunt. The first team to solve the clues wins the Founder's Brass Compass.

Kabir stares at the compass. He wants it for his satchel. He wants to hold that heavy brass.

Mr. Henderson hands out worksheets.

Kabir looks at his paper. He needs to write "trophy" on the team line.

He holds his pencil tight. He writes: T-R-O-F-E-E.

He stops. He stares. It looks wrong. The sounds do not match the letters.

He erases it. He writes: T-R-O-P-H-Y.

He sighs.

INT. CLASSROOM - LATER

CHLOE (10) looks over at Kabir's paper. She is energetic and wears bright sneakers.

CHLOE
Hey, Kabir. You spelled "scavenger" with a "j".

Kabir rubs his neck.

KABIR
I know. Writing words is hard for me.

CHLOE
Why is that?

KABIR
I have dyslexia. For me, spelling is like walking on loose gravel. My feet slip on the sounds. The letters do not match what I hear in my head.

CHLOE
Does it make reading hard too?

KABIR
Sometimes. But I can remember anything I hear once. And I can build things.

He clicks his folding ruler open. He measures his pencil.

KABIR (CONT'D)
I see shapes and spaces clearly.

EXT. SCHOOL YARD - AFTERNOON

Kabir, Chloe, and LEO (10) stand near the old brick wall. Leo holds a clipboard and a pencil.

LEO
The first clue is hidden behind the oldest brick in the courtyard.

Kabir looks at the wall. He does not look at the signs. He looks at the patterns of the bricks.

He points to a brick that is slightly recessed.

KABIR
There. The spatial alignment is off. It does not fit the pattern.

He pulls the brick out. A dusty brass plate sits inside.

The plate has written words and a geometric map.

LEO
Let me see!

Leo grabs it. He reads the words quickly.

LEO (CONT'D)
"Seek the heart of the school where the water boils. Follow the path of the silent shepherd."

Kabir looks at the plate. The letters look like a wall of text. He struggles to correlate the phonemes of the written words.

But his mind holds the sounds Leo just spoke. He remembers them perfectly.

KABIR
The boiler room. It is beneath the gym.

LEO
How do you know that?

KABIR
I heard the pipes hum when we were in the gym last week. The sound came from the north corner. The spatial layout matches this map.

INT. BASEMENT CORRIDOR - LATER

They stand in front of the heavy boiler room door. The air is cool and dusty.

They slip inside. Dusty pipes run along the ceiling.

Kabir pulls his folding ruler from his canvas satchel. He clicks it open. He measures the width of the corridor.

KABIR
The school map says this hallway is ten feet wide. But it is only eight feet here.

LEO
So what? It is just an old basement.

KABIR
So there is a hidden space behind this wall. Exactly two feet of empty space.

He taps the brick wall. It sounds hollow.

He finds a loose iron lever near the floor. He pulls it.

The brick wall swings open. A dark tunnel lies ahead.

CHLOE
Whoa! You found a secret door just by measuring?

KABIR
I see the physical space. My brain does not get stuck on the surface.

INT. TUNNELS - CONTINUOUS

They walk into the tunnel. The air smells of old earth.

They reach a fork. Two wooden doors stand before them.

Above the left door, a sign reads: GATE OF THE WRIGHT.

Above the right door, a sign reads: GATE OF THE WRITE.

Leo points to the right door.

LEO
The clue said "written words". It must be this one. "Write" means to write with a pen.

Chloe looks at the left door.

CHLOE
But what does "Wright" mean?

Kabir looks at the words.

They sound identical to him: /raɩt/. He struggles to match the spelling to the sound.

KABIR
They sound the same. But they mean different things.

LEO
The spelling tells us which is which. We should go right.

Kabir clicks his folding ruler. He looks at the geometry of the doors. He looks at the ceiling.

KABIR
No. The left door aligns with the old workshop above us. A "wright" is an old word for a builder. Like a shipwright.

LEO
But the spelling—

KABIR
The clue is a trap for people who only look at the spelling. The physical space tells the real story. The builder's gate is the true path.

He opens the left door. Inside is a safe passage.

The right door has a deep, empty pit behind it.

Leo gulps.

LEO
Okay. You were right. Or wright.

INT. THE INNER CHAMBER - LATER

They enter a large stone room. In the center sits an ancient wooden chest.

On the chest is a brass lock with three buttons. Each button has a picture and a word beneath it.

Button one: A picture of a phone. The word is PHONE.

Button two: A picture of a shepherd. The word is SHEPHERD.

Button three: A picture of a ghost. The word is GHOST.

Leo reads the inscription on the chest.

LEO
"To open the chest, press the button with the silent guide."

CHLOE
A silent guide?

LEO
It must be GHOST. The 'H' in ghost is silent. We do not say /g-h-ost/.

CHLOE
Wait. What about PHONE? The 'E' at the end is silent.

They look at Kabir.

Kabir looks at the words. He feels his chest tighten. Reading under pressure is hard.

But he focuses on the sounds. He breaks the words into their phonemes in his mind.

KABIR
No. Think about how we say the words.

LEO
What do you mean?

KABIR
In "Phone", the 'P' and 'H' join together. They make one sound: /f/. That is a digraph.

CHLOE
And "Ghost"?

KABIR
The 'G' and 'H' make the /g/ sound.

He looks at "SHEPHERD". He says the word slowly.

KABIR (CONT'D)
Shep-herd.

He claps the syllables.

KABIR (CONT'D)
We say /ʃəpərd/. We do not say /ʃəfərd/.

LEO
So?

KABIR
The 'P' and 'H' in shepherd are not a digraph. They do not make the /f/ sound. The 'H' is completely silent. It is a phonological trap. We write 'h', but we only say the 'p' and then the 'er' sound.

Leo stares at the word.

LEO
Wow. I read that word a thousand times and never realized the 'h' was silent. I just assumed 'ph' always made the /f/ sound.

KABIR
My brain has to work hard to match letters to sounds. So I notice when the sounds do not fit the rules.

Kabir presses the button for SHEPHERD.

Click. The lock springs open.

INT. THE INNER CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

The chest lid creaks open.

Inside, resting on a velvet cushion, is the Founder's Brass Compass. It gleams in the dim light.

Kabir reaches in and lifts it gently. He smiles. He feels the weight of the brass in his hand.

CHLOE
You did it, Kabir!

LEO
We would have fallen into the pit without you.

KABIR
We did it together.

He places the compass in his canvas satchel next to his folding ruler.

INT. CLASSROOM - NEXT DAY

Mr. Henderson stands at the front of the room.

MR. HENDERSON
Congratulations to Kabir, Chloe, and Leo for finding the Founder's Brass Compass!

The class applauds.

Kabir sits at his desk. He has the compass sitting next to his notebook.

He is writing a journal entry about the adventure.

He struggles to spell "adventure".

He writes: A-D-V-E-N-C-H-E-R.

He looks at it. He knows it is not quite right. He doesn't feel bad about it.

He clicks his folding ruler.

He knows his brain reads the world in its own beautiful way.

THE END